<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<appendix xml:id="app-protocol-summary">
  <title>Current Coding Protocol Summary</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This appendix is the compact operational summary of the current coding protocol.
      It does not place historical policies beside current rules. The version fields on a
      coding record determine which protocol governed that record; prior versions and
      superseded decisions belong in the research journal and repository history.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-app-protocol-status">
      <title>Status: Derived Operational Summary</title>
      <p>
        This appendix is a controlled operational summary derived from the canonical
        framework and handbook chapters and the frozen Coding Protocol <m>\pi</m>; it
        does not define rules of its own. The full explanations and examples remain in
        Parts II and III, and the field-level specification is
        <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>. When this summary appears to
        conflict with a versioned protocol supplied for a coding batch, stop and ask
        the research lead which artifact governs. Do not resolve a version conflict by
        choosing the rule you prefer.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-evidence">
    <title>Evidence Boundary</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Code the justification visible in the response. Do not claim that one short
          response reveals a student's complete conception, belief, or mental process.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Read the complete question, supplied mathematical state, requested goals, and
          assigned normative reference before interpreting the response.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Record the student's wording before paraphrasing. Do not repair omitted
          conditions, replace terminology, import a theorem, or supply a conclusion the
          student did not make.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Preserve uncertainty, competing readings, OOV language, and no-match outcomes
          as data rather than forcing the nearest convenient category.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-workflow">
    <title>Required Coding Sequence</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-app-protocol-workflow">
      <title>Current response-coding sequence.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="14%"/>
        <col width="27%"/>
        <col width="59%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Step</cell>
          <cell>Decision</cell>
          <cell>Current rule</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell>Screen</cell>
          <cell>Decide whether the response contains analyzable mathematical justification. Do not screen on correctness.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>Segment</cell>
          <cell>Identify every defensible episode before matching codes. Preserve alternative segmentations in notes.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>Transcribe</cell>
          <cell>Copy the criterion and judgment verbatim. Keep reconstructed reasoning out of verbatim fields.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell>Record the link</cell>
          <cell>Write the minimal link sufficient to license the step and classify it as <c>explicit</c>, <c>implicit</c>, or <c>absent</c>.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>5</cell>
          <cell>Relate episodes</cell>
          <cell>Mark <c>chained(k)</c> or <c>independent</c>, leaving the relation blank for a lone episode; note detected nesting. Do not collapse defensible episodes under the interim nesting rule.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>6</cell>
          <cell>Match</cell>
          <cell>Use code definitions and question-specific inferential relationships, not isolated keywords. Preserve alternate matches, OOV, and no-match cases.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>7</cell>
          <cell>Evaluate correctness</cell>
          <cell>For complete episodes, judge criterion truth and inferential validity separately.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>8</cell>
          <cell>Evaluate coverage</cell>
          <cell>Record which question goals are addressed. Do not lower a local correctness annotation because the response is incomplete overall.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>9</cell>
          <cell>Record uncertainty</cell>
          <cell>Assign coder confidence and write a specific rationale for difficult or low-confidence decisions.</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>10</cell>
          <cell>Route</cell>
          <cell>Apply the assigned disposition or escalation rule. Preserve unresolved cases and prior proposals.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-episode-rules">
    <title>Episode Rules</title>

    <dl width="medium">
      <li>
        <title>Episode form</title>
        <p>
          Each episode is <m>e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k)</m>: criterion, inferential link,
          judgment. A response is represented by the ordered sequence
          <m>E(x)=\langle e_1,\ldots,e_n\rangle</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Completeness</title>
        <p>
          An episode is complete exactly when a criterion and a judgment are
          identifiable. A missing criterion or judgment makes it incomplete and
          unscored.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Link status</title>
        <p>
          <c>explicit</c> means a link or justification is stated; <c>implicit</c> means
          the minimal link is recoverable; <c>absent</c> means no justification is
          recoverable. An absent link alone does not make an episode incomplete.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Absent links</title>
        <p>
          When no justification is recoverable, record <c>[none stated]</c> in the
          link field and classify the link as absent. Do not invent a justification.
          <c>[none stated]</c> never changes completeness and never blocks the two
          correctness digits: <m>c^1</m> and <m>c^2</m> are still evaluated from the
          criterion and judgment as written (Coding Protocol <m>\pi</m> §4). In
          practice an absent link rarely survives a valid entailment — if a standard
          principle licensed the step, the link would be recoverable and therefore
          implicit — but the evaluation, not that shortcut, is the rule.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Current nesting rule</title>
        <p>
          The final retention policy for nested or chained episodes remains open.
          Until it is frozen, record every defensible episode, mark the relation, and
          place any proposed merger in notes.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-matching">
    <title>Matching and Vocabulary Rules</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Complete a blind extraction pass without codebook access when the assigned
          study design requires it. Leave code-dependent fields blank rather than
          guessing.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Match the whole criterion-to-judgment relationship against the current
          question-specific working codebook. A shared word does not establish a match.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          When several codes plausibly fit one episode, record a primary code and all
          defensible alternatives. When separate episodes enact separate controls, code
          the episodes separately.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use <term>OOV</term> when relevant terminology or a canonical proposition is
          missing from the controlled vocabulary. Use <term>no match</term> when the
          episode can be expressed in available vocabulary but no current codebook entry
          describes the relationship adequately.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          A new term or code is a proposal, not an immediate research category. Review it
          across similar and near-miss cases before acceptance.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-correctness">
    <title>Correctness and Goal Coverage</title>

    <p>
      Every complete episode receives the pair
      <md>
        \operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c_k^1,c_k^2),
      </md>
      where <m>c_k^1</m> evaluates the criterion against the actual problem state and
      <m>c_k^2</m> evaluates whether the inferential link licenses the judgment for the
      assigned question frame.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-app-protocol-correctness">
      <title>Interpretation of the correctness pair.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="16%"/>
        <col width="84%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Pair</cell>
          <cell>Interpretation</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>(1,1)</m></cell>
          <cell>The criterion is true and the inference is valid.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>(1,0)</m></cell>
          <cell>The criterion is true, but it does not validly support the judgment.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>(0,1)</m></cell>
          <cell>The criterion is false, although the criterion-to-judgment relationship is valid as an inference pattern.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>(0,0)</m></cell>
          <cell>The criterion is false and the inference is invalid.</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>blank</cell>
          <cell>The episode is incomplete or cannot be evaluated under the assigned normative reference.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The pair evaluates a local justification, not the whole response. Record goal
      coverage separately: the identifier(s) of the question goals the episode's
      judgment addresses, <c>off-target</c> when it addresses none, or <c>unclear</c>
      with a note when it cannot be determined (Coding Protocol <m>\pi</m> §2). Goal
      coverage never removes a row and never changes a code or correctness digit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-review">
    <title>Independence, Review, and Adjudication</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          During an independent pass, do not inspect another coder's work or solicit
          their answer.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          In a tool-review pass, preserve the machine proposal. Record the human action
          as <c>confirm</c>, <c>add</c>, <c>remove</c>, <c>replace</c>, or
          <c>reject_all</c>, with a rationale.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          When coders disagree, classify the earliest divergence using the current
          taxonomy: <c>SCREEN</c>, <c>BOUNDARY</c>, <c>CRITERION</c>, <c>LINK</c>,
          <c>JUDGMENT</c>, <c>RELATION</c>, <c>MATCH</c>, <c>CORRECTNESS</c>,
          <c>COVERAGE</c>, <c>VOCABULARY</c>, <c>POLICY</c>, or <c>OTHER</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Adjudicate from the original question and response, the visible evidence, and
          the governing materials. Preserve the case as unresolved when the evidence or
          current policy does not support one defensible decision.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Test a proposed guidance revision on the motivating case, at least two similar
          cases, and at least one near miss before adoption.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-provenance">
    <title>Version and Record Discipline</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Record the governing handbook, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, and
          question-frame versions before beginning a batch.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Preserve the independent first pass, confidence notes, disagreement records,
          adjudicated result, review actions, rationales, reviewer, and date.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Do not silently revise earlier rows. Record coder drift and use an approved
          recalibration or recoding pass.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Freeze the analytic materials before formal validation. A mid-validation
          change requires a new version and an explicit decision about recoding prior
          records.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Handle student responses only in approved systems. Do not submit real response
          text to an external service unless that specific use and provider are approved.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-app-protocol-final-check">
    <title>Submission Check</title>

    <p>
      Before submitting a response or batch, verify that you:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p>used the complete question context and correct governing versions;</p></li>
      <li><p>preserved source wording and did not repair the response;</p></li>
      <li><p>recorded every defensible episode and its relation;</p></li>
      <li><p>kept extraction, matching, correctness, and coverage separate;</p></li>
      <li><p>preserved alternate matches, OOV, no-match, and unresolved cases;</p></li>
      <li><p>left incomplete episodes unscored;</p></li>
      <li><p>explained low-confidence decisions and overrides;</p></li>
      <li><p>preserved prior proposals and review history; and</p></li>
      <li><p>kept all research data in approved project systems.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The field-level companion to this summary is
      <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </section>
</appendix>
